content:
maya: Heads-up — streaming client reauth is failing when we impersonate a service account in another project. Getting an "audience mismatch" and the streaming lib doesn't retry cleanly. Anyone seen this? Using Node SDK @redwood/sdk v0.9.1, `createStream` with `auth: { impersonate: 'projects/proj-b/serviceAccounts/sa-b@proj-b.iam.gserviceaccount.com' }`

nate: @maya can you paste the full error text? Is the error from the token exchange step or from the downstream service rejecting the token? Usually this comes from using the wrong token type (signed JWT vs ID token).

maya: Error: "token rejected: aud expected 'redwood:stream' got 'https://example.com'". It happens after a reauth (rotation) — first token in session is fine, rotated token fails. No other obvious stack trace.

raj: This smells like we called `signJwt` (which sets `aud` differently) instead of `generateIdToken`. For cross-project flows you should generate an ID token with `target_audience` (or `audience`) set to whatever the downstream service expects. Quick Node example: 
```js
// use IAM Credentials generateIdToken, not signJwt
const res = await iamCredentials.generateIdToken({
  name: `projects/-/serviceAccounts/${saEmail}`,
  body: { targetAudience: CLIENT_ID, includeEmail: false }
});
const idToken = res.data.token;
```
Our SDK wrappers map params slightly differently (PR internal/1423 updates this).

nate: There's also a known reauth edge where the streaming reauth code compares `aud` equality verbatim; if the original token used a client-id and the new token uses a URL (or vice-versa) the equality check fails and the client treats it as an unrecoverable auth mismatch. Filed as RW-3187. Short-term workarounds: 1) normalize expected audience in your service to accept both forms, or 2) ensure `generateIdToken` uses the exact `aud` your service validates.

devex-bot: FYI doc + issue: https://redwood.internal/devex/auth/impersonation-oidc (see section "ID tokens vs signed JWTs"). Created issue RW-3187 and linked a small repro under devex/test-repos.

maya: Thanks — quick follow-up: in Python client is the param named `target_audience` or `audience`? I want to patch our examples so users don't mix these up.

raj: Python/google libs use `generate_id_token(name, target_audience=...)`. Node (IAM Credentials REST) uses `body.audience` in some older wrappers; our SDK mapped it to `audience` previously which caused confusion. Both approach ultimately set the `aud` claim in the produced ID token.

nate: I'll add a small unit + integration test that rotates tokens and asserts audempotent normalization (treat URL vs client-id as equivalent for now). @maya can you drop your minimal repro snippet into RW-3187 so I can wire it to the test?

maya: Will paste repro in RW-3187 in a few minutes and ping for review. Also adding a short blurb to our quickstart examples about "use generateIdToken for OIDC flows". :thumbsup:
